chx:F1 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Background"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Chapter 5 of the 3rd edition teaches four ways to find and prevent bugs: raising an exception with the raise statement and catching it with except, assertions that check a condition the programmer believes is true, the logging module with its five levels and its options for a file and for switching messages off, and the debugger of the Mu editor with its Continue, Step In, Step Over, Step Out and Stop controls and its breakpoints, closing with a buggy adding program, a coin flip program and the practice program Buggy Coin Toss Game." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01 .
chx:F2 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's printed behaviours reproduce under Python 3.14.4: box_print raises the messages the chapter shows for a width of 1 and a two-character symbol, a failing assert raises AssertionError with an empty message, the level names carry the numbers 10, 20, 30, 40 and 50, the factorial with range(n + 1) returns 0 and the fixed one 120, and the adding program prints 5342 instead of 50 until its answers are converted with int." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P06 .
chx:F3 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter's advice that assertions are for programmer errors follows from how the interpreter treats them: assert expression is equivalent to raising AssertionError only when __debug__ is true, and a program run with the -O option, or code compiled with optimisation, contains no assertion at all, so an assert must never carry a check the program needs to perform on user input." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P10 .
chx:F4 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The chapter presents logging as a replacement for print calls, and the documentation adds what makes it more than that: the default level is WARNING so debug and info calls print nothing until basicConfig lowers the threshold, basicConfig does nothing when the root logger already has handlers unless force=True is given, logging.disable(level) silences that level and every lower one until it is called with NOTSET, and a call that passes arguments to the message formats them only if the message is emitted." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P05, chx:P06 .
chx:F5 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Comparative analysis"@en ; sen0414:findingText "The debugger the chapter describes belongs to the Mu editor, whose five controls correspond to commands of the standard library debugger pdb - step, next, return, continue and break - so the same technique is available in any Python installation, and since Python 3.7 a program can start it with the built-in breakpoint(), which calls sys.breakpointhook and is switched off by setting PYTHONBREAKPOINT to 0." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P07, chx:P08, chx:P10, chx:P12 .
chx:F6 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Python 3.14 added to debugging a safe way to attach the debugger to a running process, python -m pdb -p followed by the process identifier, built on sys.remote_exec of PEP 768, and lets a hardcoded breakpoint() reuse the last debugger instance so that its breakpoints and settings persist between stops." ; sen0414:cites chx:P11, chx:P15, chx:P08 .
chx:F7 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Contemporary developments"@en ; sen0414:findingText "Since Python 3.11 a traceback points at the failing expression inside a line with carets, so the failing subscript in a chained lookup can be seen without a debugger, and any exception can carry notes added with add_note, which are printed after the traceback and kept in __notes__, so code that catches and re-raises can say where in the work the error occurred." ; sen0414:cites chx:P13, chx:P14, chx:P03, chx:P04 .
chx:F8 a sen0414:Finding ; rdfs:label "Conclusion"@en ; sen0414:findingText "For an advanced course, chapter 5 is best taught as a choice among four instruments by the question asked: raise when the caller can do something about the error, assert when the programmer's belief must hold, log when the run must leave a record, and step through with the debugger when the state at one point is the question; the chapter's printed outputs hold under 3.14.4, and optimised mode, lazy formatting, force=True, breakpoint(), remote attachment, fine-grained tracebacks and exception notes are what the chapter leaves for the course to add." ; sen0414:cites chx:P01, chx:P02, chx:P05, chx:P07, chx:P11 .
chx:Scorecard a sen0414:QualityScorecard ; rdfs:label "Quality scorecard"@en ; sen0414:topicCoverage "27 primary-source concepts inventoried from the textbook ontology." ; sen0414:citationStrength "15 sources, all opened and verified at 2026-09-29T08:06:45; 14 cited by findings." ; sen0414:methodologyCompliance "RDODI procedure v1.6.0 Stage 1." ; sen0414:reproducibility "Every source is a stable public URL; every claim names its sources." .
